    "durationMs": 119727,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Structure**\n- `main.go` — reads `DATABASE_URL`, connects a pgx pool, applies migrations, serves chi on `0.0.0.0:8000`\n- `internal/store/` — pgx-backed persistence (Create/Get/IncrementClicks/List) plus an embedded-SQL migration runner tracked in a `schema_migrations` table\n- `internal/api/api.go` — chi handlers with URL validation (absolute http/https with host), code validation (`^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`), and crypto/rand generated codes\n- `start.sh` — rebuilds the binary only if missing, then runs it (migrations apply at startup before serving); deps were fetched during development\n- `README.md` — startup instructions\n\n**Verified end-to-end against every requirement**\n- POST /links: 201 with `clicks: 0`, custom and generated codes, URL echoed unchanged\n- Duplicate custom code → 409; malformed JSON, bad/missing URL, bad code → 422\n- GET /links/{code} → 200; GET /r/{code} → 302 with correct Location, clicks incremented exactly once per hit (atomic `UPDATE ... RETURNING`)\n- GET /links → ascending creation order with `items` + `total`\n- Unknown codes → 404 with `error` field\n- Killed and restarted via `sh start.sh` — data and click counts survived\n- Test rows were truncated afterward so the suite starts with a clean database\n\nchi stays at v5.2.1 (pgx v5.7.6 was pinned since newer versions require Go 1.25), and `go vet`/build are clean."
  }
